This page is almost illegible. It appears to be a faint, unreversed offset (ghost) impression of the next sheet, page '2', rather than independent content. The layout matches page 2 line for line:

- a cube-shaped diagram of topoi with a brace labelled 'morphismes de topos annelés [⇢ défini si X de torsion sur Z]';
- the boxed 'NB | S sous-entendu dans notations';
- the paragraphs on arrows to and from X_zar;
- the Chern-class paragraph.

The page carries the archival number '1'. Reconstructed from the legible shapes plus page 2, the mathematics is as follows.

For X over a base S, Grothendieck lists several ringed topoi attached to X:
- X_zar, the Zariski topos;
- X_conn, modules with integrable connection;
- X_P-conn, a divided-power ('P' = PD) variant of connections;
- X_strat, stratifications;
- X_cris, crystals on thickenings without divided powers, or with nilpotent conditions;
- X_P-cris, the PD-crystalline topos;
- X_inf, the infinitesimal topos.

The morphisms of ringed topoi between them form a cube-like lattice. Stated comparisons on page 2:
- conn → strat is an equivalence in characteristic 0;
- conn → cris and P-conn → P-cris are equivalences when X/S is smooth;
- the dashed PD-arrows exist only when X is torsion over Z, so that divided powers on (p) make sense.

The arrows to and from X_zar give localization isomorphisms, spectral sequences, and the exact sequence 0 → J → O_𝒳 → O_{𝒳_0} → 0.

For an invertible sheaf L on X_zar there is a Chern class c'(L) in H^2 of X_cris and of X_P-cris. It comes from the extension 1 → 1+J → O^* → O_0^* → 1, via log(1+J) ≅ J under divided powers. Its vanishing is the necessary and sufficient obstruction for L to extend to an invertible crystal. In the smooth case this means L admits an integrable connection.

The purpose is to set up the general comparison framework for 'Crystals and the de Rham cohomology of schemes' (the 1966 IHES lectures, published in Dix exposés).
